# Line endings are part of the contract here, not a preference.
#
# Every lab runs the repo's shell scripts inside a Linux container. Git for Windows defaults to
# `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout — and a `.sh` file with CRLF fails in
# bash with `set: -: invalid option` and `$'\r': command not found`, which reads like a corrupt
# script rather than a line-ending problem. WSL is not a defence: the clone is still made by a Git
# that applied the conversion.
#
# `eol=lf` forces LF in the WORKING TREE on every platform, overriding any local core.autocrlf, so a
# student never has to configure anything for the containers to start.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files that actually execute.
# The one exception: cmd reads a batch file reliably only with CRLF.
*.cmd     text eol=crlf
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.bash    text eol=lf
justfile  text eol=lf
Makefile  text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf

# Never touch these.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.ico     binary
*.woff    binary
*.woff2   binary
*.jsonl   -text
*.patch   -text
